                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_portfolio:     `<strong>Right frame.</strong> Total reward is a portfolio, not a salary number. The same euro paid as base, variable or benefits does three different things — and that is exactly what lets you serve three segments that want opposite things inside one envelope.`,
                    coach_r1_flatraise:     `<strong>A flat across-the-board raise feels fair and fixes nothing.</strong> In a 5%-margin retailer you've permanently inflated fixed cost, the warehouse gap is still there, the specialists are still 10% below market and leaving, and managers are no more motivated. What does the <em>mix</em> let you do that a flat raise can't?`,
                    coach_r1_equityonly:    `<strong>The equity scandal is real — but it isn't the whole problem.</strong> Fix only the warehouse gap and your specialists keep resigning at +22% and your managers stay flat. Equity is one constraint inside a wider total-reward redesign.`,
                    coach_r1_market:        `<strong>Matching the market for specialists is necessary — but not first and not alone.</strong> Chase only the specialists and the works-council grievance escalates while frontline fairness collapses. Three claims, one envelope.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed reward as a portfolio AND accepted that ~€8.3M of legitimate claims must be sequenced into a €5.04M envelope. That is the discipline the rest of the design depends on.`,
                    coach_r1_everyone:      `<strong>“Fund everyone fully” is how you breach +3%.</strong> The extra €3.3M doesn't exist; pretending it does just defers the trade-off you'll be forced to make anyway. Sequence the claims deliberately.`,
                    coach_r1_unfair:        `<strong>That misreads the motivation failure.</strong> Differential variable pay isn't unfair — pay <em>with no line-of-sight</em> is what fails, because top and weak managers then earn nearly the same bonus. The fix is line-of-sight, not flattening.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>You're redesigning without the numbers.</strong> Without quantifying the 14% equity gap and the ~10% market gap in euros, you can't size the levers — you'll guess, and the works council will out-evidence you.`,
                    coach_r2_basebenefits:  `<strong>Good segment-fit for frontline.</strong> A base correction fixes the warehouse equity gap and cost-efficient benefits land with hourly staff who value security — the right euro for the right segment.`,
                    coach_r2_allbase:       `<strong>All-base for frontline is safe but blunt.</strong> It corrects equity yet locks in permanent fixed cost and does nothing motivational. Pairing the base fix with benefits stretches the same euro further for this segment.`,
                    coach_r2_frontvariable: `<strong>Pushing variable pay onto hourly staff backfires.</strong> Frontline staff value base security and benefits; performance pay feels risky and unfair to them and won't fix the equity gap. Wrong lever for this segment.`,
                    coach_r2_specbenefits:  `<strong>Benefits are cheap per euro — but invisible to specialists.</strong> Market-priced people weigh cash, not perks. Funding them mostly through benefits leaves them 10% below market and still being poached at +22%.`,
                    coach_r2_spectoken:     `<strong>A token raise won't hold the specialists.</strong> They're already fielding offers 22% above base, and each exit costs ~€48,000 to replace. Underpaying the segment the market is actively bidding for is the fastest-growing cost.`,
                    coach_r2_starvespec:    `<strong>You've over-funded frontline and starved the specialists.</strong> Equity is moving but retention isn't — the people the market is bidding for got almost nothing. Rebalance toward the specialists' cash gap.`,
                    coach_r2_mgmttoohigh:   `<strong>Pushing management variable far above the 18–22% norm inflates cost.</strong> Without genuine line-of-sight (Round 3) a bigger bonus just costs more without differentiating performers. Hit the norm, then design the line-of-sight.`,
                    coach_r2_overspend:     `<strong>You've breached the +3% envelope.</strong> In a 5%-margin retailer, overspend wipes out a slice of net profit and the CFO will veto it. The envelope is a hard cap — which claim do you partly defer?`,
                    coach_r3_phased:        `<strong>Phasing the equity fix is the credible move.</strong> Close enough now to satisfy the works council and commit a funded path for the rest — you remediate the breach without letting it eat ~40% of the envelope and starve the specialists.`,
                    coach_r3_fullnow:       `<strong>You closed the warehouse gap 100% now — the council is happy.</strong> But it ate ~40% of the envelope, the specialists got almost nothing, and two more just resigned at +22%. You fixed internal equity and lost external competitiveness.`,
                    coach_r3_defer:         `<strong>Deferring the equity fix is dangerous.</strong> The works council demands equal pay for equal work now; a vague promise of a review next year triggers the collective grievance you're trying to avoid. Close it credibly, even if phased.`,
                    coach_r3_both:          `<strong>That's the reconciliation.</strong> Holding the equity ratio AND market-matching specialists via a defensible premium rule is how you serve internal equity and external competitiveness at once — the central Round 3 tension, resolved.`,
                    coach_r3_internal:      `<strong>Internal equity alone loses the specialists.</strong> Pay everyone the same band regardless of market and the people the market is bidding for keep leaving at +22%. Equal pay for equal work doesn't mean ignoring a scarce-skills premium.`,
                    coach_r3_external:      `<strong>Chasing the market alone re-opens the equity breach.</strong> Let the equity ratio slip to pay specialists and the works council escalates the grievance. You need a rule that defends both, not a choice between them.`,
                    coach_r3_impose:        `<strong>Technically elegant, but you didn't negotiate it.</strong> The works council just rejected it and the April pay review is now a dispute. Costa Solé's point is that reward is a labour-relations act, not a spreadsheet. What did you reserve to trade?`,
                    coach_r3_capitulate:    `<strong>Giving the council everything isn't negotiation.</strong> Fully funding equity and dropping the rest buys peace on one front and loses the specialists and managers. Negotiate from the data and reserve a concession to trade.`,
                    coach_r3_noline:        `<strong>Variable pay with no line-of-sight doesn't motivate.</strong> Without a clear link between effort, results and reward, your best and weakest managers earn nearly the same bonus — you've spent on variable pay and bought no differentiation.`,
                    coach_r4_affordable:    `<strong>Fair AND affordable is the board-ready case.</strong> Holding +3%, closing equity to an acceptable ratio, stemming attrition and tying bonus to results is a package the CFO can sign and the council can accept.`,
                    coach_r4_overspend:     `<strong>The full €8.3M package against a €5.04M envelope will be vetoed.</strong> In a 5%-margin retailer that overspend wipes out a third of net profit. Which segment's claim do you partly defer, and how do you sequence it over two years?`,
                    coach_r4_redistribute:  `<strong>Same total, moved around, is not a motivational design.</strong> If you only redistribute without line-of-sight and segment-fit, the mix does nothing a flat raise couldn't — you've reshuffled cost, not engineered reward.`,
                    coach_r4_budgetonly:    `<strong>Budget adherence alone is a thin scorecard.</strong> The CFO cares about the number, but a package judged only on cost can't show whether equity, retention or motivation actually moved. Commit the four metrics.`,
                    coach_r4_noscorecard:   `<strong>No scorecard means no accountability.</strong> “Trust the design” is how reward decisions drift; without committed metrics you can't show the board the package worked or course-correct next cycle.`,
                    coach_r4_phaseplan:     `<strong>Naming what you deferred is the honest, durable move.</strong> Committing a funded two-year sequence for the partly-deferred claim keeps the works council on side and turns a hard trade-off into a credible plan.`,
                    coach_r4_defernothing:  `<strong>Claiming the envelope covered everything isn't true.</strong> €5.04M funds ~60% of ~€8.3M of claims; pretending otherwise loses credibility with a council that has the spreadsheet. Name what was deferred and phase it.`,
                    coach_r4_defersilent:   `<strong>Deferring quietly will be discovered.</strong> Not telling the council what wasn't funded breaks the trust the whole package depends on — and they leaked the first spreadsheet. Be transparent about the phasing.`,

